Jenkins CICD Pipeline + Azure VM + Azure AKS Lab
This lab demonstrates an end-to-end CI/CD workflow on Azure using Ubuntu VM + Jenkins + GitHub/Bitbucket + Maven + Docker + Docker Hub + Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS).
You will:
•	Prepare an Ubuntu VM with all required DevOps tools
•	Configure Jenkins and integrate with GitHub/Bitbucket, Maven, Docker
•	Build a Java application, containerize it, and push the image to Docker Hub
•	Deploy the containerized application to AKS and expose it publicly
Target Audience:
•	DevOps beginners to intermediate
•	Jenkins, Docker, Kubernetes, Azure learners
•	Ideal for classroom / demo / certification-aligned labs
Architecture:
GitHub/Bitbucket Repo
↓
Jenkins (Azure VM)
↓ Build (Maven)
↓ Docker Build image
↓ Push image to Docker Hub
↓
Azure AKS
↓
Public Service (LoadBalancer)
Prerequisites
•	Azure Subscription
•	GitHub account
•	Docker Hub account
•	Basic Linux commands knowledge
•	Sample Java Spring Boot / Java Maven app





LAB 1: Create Azure Ubuntu VM
Objective: Provision an Ubuntu server to host Jenkins and DevOps tools.
Steps:
a. Login to Azure Portal
b. Create Virtual Machine
•	Image: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS
•	Size: Standard B2s
•	Authentication: Username + SSH / Password
c. Open Inbound Ports
•	22 (SSH)
•	8080 (Jenkins / App – optional)
Add the in-bound port:
Field  	Value
Source  	Any Source
port ranges	*
Destination	AnyDestination 
port ranges	8080
Protocol	TCP
Action    	Allow
Priority  	1000
Name    	Allow-Jenkins-8080
d. Connect to VM using:
ssh azureuser@<public-ip>
o/p: provide the password

LAB 2: Install Tools on Ubuntu
a. Update System
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
b. Install Git
sudo apt install git -y
git --version
c. Install Maven
sudo apt install maven -y
mvn -version 
o/p: maven and jdk 21 path
d. Install OpenJDK 17 for the springboot app as it support jdk 17
sudo apt install openjdk-17-jdk -y
java -version
path: /usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk-amd64
	Optional: Select the jdk 21 or 17: sudo update-alternatives --config java
	o/p: select the version to set it default for vm.		
e. Docker installation:
i. Install Docker
sudo apt install docker.io -y
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
ii. Provide permission on docker.sock:
sudo chmod 666 /var/run/docker.sock
(Re-login required)
iii. Verify Docker
docker –version
docker run hello-world
f. Jenkins installation: 
i. Install Jenkins
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc \
  https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2026.key
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc]" \
  https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee \
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null
sudo apt update
sudo apt install jenkins
ii. Start Jenkins:
sudo systemctl status jenkins
o/p expected: started
sudo systemctl start jenkins
iii. Access Jenkins:
http://<VM-Public-IP>:8080 
iv. Unlock Jenkins:
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword

